{
  "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
  "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Tuesday, January 13, 2009 The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:14 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Donald B. Verrilli, Jr.",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The question in this case is whether Petitioner Montejo should be denied the Sixth Amendment protections of Michigan v. Jackson because he silently acquiesced in the appointment of counsel at his initial hearing, rather than affirmatively accepting the appointment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0000-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Louisiana Supreme Court believed that an affirmative acceptance was required to trigger Jackson and therefore upheld the admission of a confession elicited during police-initiated interrogation after Montejo's Sixth Amendment right had attached and after a lawyer had been appointed to represent him."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I thought that the rationale of Jackson was that the confession is simply deemed to be coerced if the defendant has expressed -- has expressed -- his desire to have counsel present or even to be represented by counsel."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Donald B. Verrilli, Jr.",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are two options: One is to treat that defendant as having elected the right to rely on counsel, and --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Donald B. Verrilli, Jr.",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0000-0006",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Detective Hall, the only officer who testified, testified that he was not aware."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Mr. Verrilli, do you think that Michigan v. Jackson is immune from being reexamined at this point?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. VERRILLI",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. VERRILLI: I think it ought not be reexamined here, Justice Alito, for several reasons. One, the Respondent has not asked for it. Two, there's a special justification that has to be shown to overrule it, as Dickerson says, in the Miranda context, and this is quite parallel."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: We wouldn't be saying that. We would just be saying that it is unrealistic to think that a confession is coerced simply because the police initiated the conversation so long as he said: Okay, I'll speak without my counsel present."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Did he have an opportunity to do it at the so-called 72-hour hearing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. LANDRY",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. LANDRY: I would respectfully disagree, Your Honor, because in Patterson the Court said that the Miranda rights were sufficient to apprise a defendant of his post-attachment Sixth Amendment rights."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But you were saying there had to be a request. You abandon that? There doesn't have to be a request?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Landry",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Petitioner has argued that the mere appointment of counsel with nothing further by this defendant constituted the request necessary under Jackson to invoke the rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I understood you to concede that if he had made that request at the 72-hour hearing, the outcome of this case would be different."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0002-0005",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If he had stood in the courtroom in Tennessee, having been told that counsel was appointed for him and had said, yes, thank you, I accept that lawyer, that that would have been sufficient to satisfy Jackson, and that would have made the difference in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0003-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And that's fully as much of a deception as telling somebody he doesn't have a lawyer when he does, or withholding information that made a big difference for Sixth Amendment purposes, which is why the Court in Moran drew that line very sharply and said, for Fifth Amendment purposes, the Sixth Amendment right hasn't attached, there isn't an interference with the attorney-client relationship, but the very same thing would be forbidden under the Sixth Amendment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1529",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "ab7435d6fc68ceb88cf64359f87b909d54dd040bc28cb141c76d820877092931",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab7435d6fc68-0003-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
